config.prompt:
Design ONE scenario that would catch a regression in this commit, as a user would hit it.

Commit: {{item.sha}}
Subject: {{item.subject}}
Why it matters: {{item.rationale}}
Changed paths:
{{item.paths}}

Read the diff for these paths before you design anything. A scenario built on what the commit probably did is worse than no scenario, because it passes for the wrong reason and you stop looking.

The scenario MUST mutate state. Opening a page and reading it is not a scenario — every regression this loop exists to catch survives a render check. Send the message, create the item, toggle the setting, cancel the run: do the thing, then go look at whether it actually landed, in the UI and in the backend.

Build in, where the diff calls for it:
- fault injection, if it touches an error path — make the failure happen rather than assuming the handler is fine
- a restart/resume arc, if it touches persistence — mutate, restart the gateway, confirm the state survived
- a resource-growth assertion, if it touches a cache or a queue — do the thing N times and check what grows
- backend inspection, always — the UI saying it worked is a claim, not evidence

Entry surface means the URL and the element a user starts from, not a description of the feature.

Return JSON: {"scenario_id": string, "title": string, "entry_surface": string, "steps": [string], "mutation": string, "backend_check": string, "fault_injection": string, "restart_arc": string, "growth_assertion": string}

Run this scenario against the running instance at {{inputs.base_url}} and report what happened.

Scenario: {{nodes.scenario-gen.output.title}}
Entry surface: {{nodes.scenario-gen.output.entry_surface}}
Steps:
{{nodes.scenario-gen.output.steps}}
The mutation that must happen: {{nodes.scenario-gen.output.mutation}}
Backend check: {{nodes.scenario-gen.output.backend_check}}

Drive the browser through the Chrome DevTools MCP server. Open a NEW page rather than taking over one the user may be looking at. Use the terminal for the backend check.

Things about this repo that will otherwise cost you the run:
- A backend .py change does NOT hot-reload. If the commit touched Python and you did not restart the gateway, you are testing the old code and your PASS means nothing.
- The frontend is served live through a `static/dist` SYMLINK. After a rebuild, hard-reload the page — a cached bundle looks exactly like a change that did not land.
- React inputs do not see a raw value assignment. Type, do not set.

Capture as you go, under the run workspace: `screenshots/*.png` at every state change, and a screen recording at `recording.mp4`.

A scenario FAILS when the mutation did not land, when the backend disagrees with the UI, or when anything you did not expect appeared in the console or the logs. "It looked right" is not a pass.

Return JSON: {"passed": boolean, "observed": string, "failure_detail": string, "repro_steps": [string]}

Propose a fix for the failure this run found, on a branch of its own.

Commit under test: {{item.sha}}
What failed: {{nodes.execute.output.failure_detail}}
What was observed: {{nodes.execute.output.observed}}
Reproduction:
{{nodes.execute.output.repro_steps}}

Work on the branch `{{nodes.evidence.output.fix_branch}}` — it has already been opened off the failing commit for you (not checked out, not pushed). Do NOT merge it and do NOT push it — a human reviews this diff before either happens, and a pushed branch takes that choice away.

Fix the cause, not the symptom. If the reproduction passes because you changed the assertion rather than the behaviour, you have made the next failure invisible; say you could not fix it instead.

Return JSON: {"branch": string, "fixed": boolean, "diff_summary": string, "why_not": string}
